      "text": "To find the integer values of \\(a\\) such that the polynomial \\(x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = 0\\) has at least one integer root, we will use the Rational Root Theorem. The Rational Root Theorem states that any potential rational root, expressed as a fraction \\( \\frac{p}{q} \\), must have \\( p \\) as a factor of the constant term (7) and \\( q \\) as a factor of the leading coefficient (1). Therefore, the possible rational roots are \\( \\pm 1, \\pm 7 \\).\n\nWe will test these possible roots by substituting them into the polynomial and checking if the polynomial evaluates to zero.\n\n1. Test \\( x = 1 \\):\n\\[\n1^3 + 3(1)^2 + a(1) + 7 = 1 + 3 + a + 7 = 11 + a\n\\]\nFor \\( x = 1 \\) to be a root, \\( 11 + a = 0 \\). Solving for \\( a \\):\n\\[\na = -11\n\\]\n\n2. Test \\( x = -1 \\):\n\\[\n(-1)^3 + 3(-1)^2 + a(-1) + 7 = -1 + 3 - a + 7 = 9 - a\n\\]\nFor \\( x = -1 \\) to be a root, \\( 9 - a = 0 \\). Solving for \\( a \\):\n\\[\na = 9\n\\]\n\n3. Test \\( x = 7 \\):\n\\[\n7^3 + 3(7)^2 + a(7) + 7 = 343 + 147 + 7a + 7 = 497 + 7a\n\\]\nFor \\( x = 7 \\) to be a root, \\( 497 + 7a = 0 \\). Solving for \\( a \\):\n\\[\n7a = -497 \\implies a = -71\n\\]\n\n4. Test \\( x = -7 \\):\n\\[\n(-7)^3 + 3(-7)^2 + a(-7) + 7 = -343 + 147 - 7a + 7 = -199 - 7a\n\\]\nFor \\( x = -7 \\) to be a root, \\( -199 - 7a = 0 \\). Solving for \\( a \\):\n\\[\n-7a = 199 \\implies a = -\\frac{199}{7}\n\\]\nSince \\(-\\frac{199}{7}\\) is not an integer, \\( x = -7 \\) is not a root.\n\nThus, the possible integer values of \\( a \\) are \\( -11, 9, \\) and \\( -71 \\). Therefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-11, 9, -71}\n\\]",
