      "text": "To find all integer values of \\( a \\) such that the polynomial \\( f(x) = x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 \\) has at least one integer root, we can use the Rational Root Theorem. The Rational Root Theorem states that any potential rational root of the polynomial \\( f(x) \\) is a factor of the constant term (7) divided by a factor of the leading coefficient (1). Therefore, the possible integer roots of the polynomial are \\( \\pm 1, \\pm 7 \\).\n\nLet's test each of these possible roots one by one.\n\n1. **Testing \\( x = 1 \\):**\n   \\[\n   f(1) = 1^3 + 3 \\cdot 1^2 + a \\cdot 1 + 7 = 1 + 3 + a + 7 = 11 + a\n   \\]\n   For \\( x = 1 \\) to be a root, \\( f(1) \\) must be zero:\n   \\[\n   11 + a = 0 \\implies a = -11\n   \\]\n\n2. **Testing \\( x = -1 \\):**\n   \\[\n   f(-1) = (-1)^3 + 3 \\cdot (-1)^2 + a \\cdot (-1) + 7 = -1 + 3 - a + 7 = 9 - a\n   \\]\n   For \\( x = -1 \\) to be a root, \\( f(-1) \\) must be zero:\n   \\[\n   9 - a = 0 \\implies a = 9\n   \\]\n\n3. **Testing \\( x = 7 \\):**\n   \\[\n   f(7) = 7^3 + 3 \\cdot 7^2 + a \\cdot 7 + 7 = 343 + 147 + 7a + 7 = 497 + 7a\n   \\]\n   For \\( x = 7 \\) to be a root, \\( f(7) \\) must be zero:\n   \\[\n   497 + 7a = 0 \\implies 7a = -497 \\implies a = -71\n   \\]\n\n4. **Testing \\( x = -7 \\):**\n   \\[\n   f(-7) = (-7)^3 + 3 \\cdot (-7)^2 + a \\cdot (-7) + 7 = -343 + 147 - 7a + 7 = -199 - 7a\n   \\]\n   For \\( x = -7 \\) to be a root, \\( f(-7) \\) must be zero:\n   \\[\n   -199 - 7a = 0 \\implies -7a = 199 \\implies a = -\\frac{199}{7}\n   \\]\n   Since \\( a \\) must be an integer, \\( a = -\\frac{199}{7} \\) is not a valid solution.\n\nThe possible integer values of \\( a \\) are \\( -11, 9, \\) and \\( -71 \\). Therefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-11, 9, -71}\n\\]",
