      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Let $r$ be an integer root of the polynomial $x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = 0.$ By the Factor Theorem, we know that $(x-r)$ is a factor of the polynomial. Thus, we can write \\[x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = (x-r)(x^2 + px + q)\\]for some integers $p$ and $q.$ Expanding the right side, we get \\[x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = x^3 + (p-r)x^2 + (q-pr)x - rq.\\]By comparing coefficients, we have \\begin{align*}\np - r &= 3, \\\\\nq - pr &= a, \\\\\n-r &= 7.\n\\end{align*}From the third equation, we have $r = -7.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get $p - (-7) = 3,$ so $p = -4.$ Substituting these values into the second equation, we get $q - (-4)(-7) = a,$ so $q - 28 = a.$ Therefore, $q = a + 28$ and $r = -7.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the polynomial, we have \\[x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = (x + 7)(x^2 - 4x + a + 28).\\]Expanding the right side, we get \\[x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = x^3 - 4x^2 + (a + 28)x - 28x + 28 + 196.\\]Combining like terms, we have \\[x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = x^3 - 4x^2 + (a + 28)x + 224.\\]Equating coefficients, we get \\[a + 28 = 7\\]and $a = -21.$ Therefore, the only integer value of $a$ that satisfies the equation is $\\boxed{-21}.$The answer is: -21",
