      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find all integer values of \\(a\\) so that the polynomial \\(P(x) = x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7\\) has at least one integer root, we start by using the Rational Root Theorem. The Rational Root Theorem states that any potential rational root, expressed in its lowest terms \\(p/q\\), must be a factor of the constant term (7) divided by a factor of the leading coefficient (1). Therefore, the possible integer roots are \\(\\pm 1, \\pm 7\\).\n\nWe will test these possible roots by substituting them into the polynomial \\(P(x)\\).\n\n1. **Testing \\(x = 1\\):**\n   \\[\n   P(1) = 1^3 + 3 \\cdot 1^2 + a \\cdot 1 + 7 = 1 + 3 + a + 7 = 11 + a\n   \\]\n   For \\(x = 1\\) to be a root, \\(P(1) = 0\\). Therefore,\n   \\[\n   11 + a = 0 \\implies a = -11\n   \\]\n\n2. **Testing \\(x = -1\\):**\n   \\[\n   P(-1) = (-1)^3 + 3 \\cdot (-1)^2 + a \\cdot (-1) + 7 = -1 + 3 - a + 7 = 9 - a\n   \\]\n   For \\(x = -1\\) to be a root, \\(P(-1) = 0\\). Therefore,\n   \\[\n   9 - a = 0 \\implies a = 9\n   \\]\n\n3. **Testing \\(x = 7\\):**\n   \\[\n   P(7) = 7^3 + 3 \\cdot 7^2 + a \\cdot 7 + 7 = 343 + 147 + 7a + 7 = 497 + 7a\n   \\]\n   For \\(x = 7\\) to be a root, \\(P(7) = 0\\). Therefore,\n   \\[\n   497 + 7a = 0 \\implies 7a = -497 \\implies a = -71\n   \\]\n\n4. **Testing \\(x = -7\\):**\n   \\[\n   P(-7) = (-7)^3 + 3 \\cdot (-7)^2 + a \\cdot (-7) + 7 = -343 + 147 - 7a + 7 = -193 - 7a\n   \\]\n   For \\(x = -7\\) to be a root, \\(P(-7) = 0\\). Therefore,\n   \\[\n   -193 - 7a = 0 \\implies 7a = -193 \\implies a = -\\frac{193}{7}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\frac{193}{7}\\) is not an integer, \\(a = -\\frac{193}{7}\\) is not a valid solution.\n\nThe integer values of \\(a\\) that make the polynomial \\(x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = 0\\) have at least one integer root are \\(-11, 9, -71\\). Therefore, the solution is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-11, 9, -71}\n\\]",
